The keeper positions on a line just outside the off stump for a right-handed batter, so that:
- the ball is taken in front of the body rather than across it;
- the view of the ball is not obstructed by the batter's body;
- the natural line of an outside edge carries towards the gloves or the slip cordon.
Depth is judged by bowler pace and bounce: the ball should arrive at a comfortable height, ideally between knee and chest, at the point of collection.

Standing up is used for:
- all spin bowling;
- medium-pace bowling where the batter might otherwise use the crease freely;
- tactical situations where a batter is repeatedly leaving the crease, even against faster bowling;
- the final delivery situations where a stumping is the only realistic dismissal.

The keeper feeds back on:
- how much the ball is swinging or seaming, and whether it is still doing so;
- the length that is causing the batter difficulty;
- the batter's trigger movement and whether they are moving across or staying leg side;
- how much the spinner is turning it relative to the previous over.

Short leg and silly point stand within a few metres of the batter, usually against spin. They must:
- wear a helmet with a grille and, typically, a shin guard and abdominal protection;
- watch the bat face and the front pad, expecting a deflection rather than a struck shot;
- crouch low, since bat-pad deflections usually travel below waist height;
- have a rehearsed evasion action for a full-blooded sweep or pull.

International scheduling is governed by a Future Tours Programme, which sets out bilateral commitments across a multi-year cycle. The central tension in modern scheduling is the competition for calendar space between:
1. bilateral international cricket in three formats;
2. ICC global events;
3. domestic franchise T20 leagues;
4. player workload and injury management.
The practical result has been increasing squad rotation, format specialisation, and in some cases players declining central contracts in favour of franchise commitments.

For a delivery to be legal:
- some part of the bowler's front foot, whether grounded or raised, must be behind the popping crease;
- the back foot must land within and not touching the return crease;
- the arm must not straighten beyond the permitted tolerance (15 degrees of elbow extension) during the delivery swing;
- the ball must be within the batter's reach and must not be dangerous.

The batting average divides by dismissals , not innings. A batter who is frequently not out therefore has an inflated average relative to their run-scoring volume. This particularly affects:
- lower-order batters who are often stranded when the innings ends;
- limited-overs finishers who bat at 6 or 7 and are regularly unbeaten;
100
- Test number 11s.

A batting average treats all runs as equal. In practice:
- 40 runs on a difficult seaming pitch may be worth more than 90 on a flat one;
- 40 in a successful chase is worth more than 40 in a lost cause;
- 40 at a strike rate of 60 in a T20 may actively harm the team.
